::PC_U03_L08_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L08_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.7
Question:
What is 180 degrees in radians?
{
=A. pi#Correct. 180 degrees equals pi radians.
~B. 2pi#pi/2 is 90 degrees.
~C. pi/2#This multiplies instead of converting.
~D. 360pi#
}
::PC_U03_L08_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L08_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.8; MLA.PC.TR.9
Question:
At pi/6, which pair gives cosine and sine?
{
~A. (1/2, sqrt(3)/2)#This reverses sine and cosine.
=B. (sqrt(3)/2, 1/2)#Correct. Unit circle points are (cosine, sine), so pi/6 gives (sqrt(3)/2, 1/2).
~C. (1, 0)#This is pi/2.
~D. (0, 1)#
}
::PC_U03_L08_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L08_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.10; MLA.PC.TR.11
Question:
Which statement is correct?
{
~A. cos(pi - x)=cos(x)#Cosine changes sign in Quadrant II.
~B. tan(90 degrees)=1#Tangent is undefined at 90 degrees.
=C. sin(pi - x)=sin(x)#Correct. pi - x is in Quadrant II, where sine stays positive.
~D. sec(pi)=1#
}
::PC_U03_L08_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L08_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.12; MLA.PC.TR.13
Question:
For y=3sin(x)+2, what is the midline?
{
~A. y=3#3 is amplitude.
~B. y=0#The graph has been shifted up.
~C. y=5#5 is the maximum, not midline.
=D. y=2#Correct. The +2 shifts the midline to y=2.
}
::PC_U03_L08_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L08_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.14
Question:
Why should a real-world trigonometric model include constraints?
{
=A. The model may only apply over a meaningful time or context.#Correct. Context determines where interpretation is meaningful.
~B. Trigonometric functions cannot be graphed.#Sine and cosine are periodic.
~C. Sine and cosine are never periodic.#Many trig values are defined.
~D. All trig values are undefined.#
}